+  {
+    "body": "## Problem\n\nHive Mind commands kept dying at startup while loading `command-stream`, in two\nalternating shapes. From the two logs attached to the issue — the *same* command,\n`fix https://github.com/link-assistant/agent --ci-cd --attach-logs --verbose`, one\nimage apart:\n\n```text\n# 2.11.1\nnpm error ENOTEMPTY: directory not empty, rmdir '.../command-stream-v-latest/examples'\nFailed to install command-stream@latest globally into '...' after 3 attempts.\n```\n\n```text\n# 2.10.2\nFailed to import module from '.../command-stream-v-latest/src/$.mjs?use-m-retry=1'.\nCaused by: Cannot find module '.../command-stream-v-latest/src/shell-parser.mjs'\n```\n\nThe missing file changed every run (`shell-parser.mjs`, `terminal-capture.mjs`,\n`$.trace.mjs`), and `npm pack command-stream@0.17.2 --dry-run --json` shows all of\nthem are published — so this was never a bad release.\n\n## Root cause\n\n`use()` runs one `npm install -g <alias>@npm:<package>@<version>` per call and has\n**no in-flight deduplication**. Node evaluates sibling top-level-await subgraphs\nconcurrently ([proved locally](experiments/issue-2113/prove-top-level-await-concurrency.mjs):\npeak concurrency 3 of 3), and 38 modules under `src/` load `command-stream` through\n`use()`, 31 of them at top level — so a cold container running `fix` launches **six\nsimultaneous global installs of one directory**. npm does not lock the global prefix,\nso those installs delete and re-extract each other's trees: the loser gets `ENOTEMPTY`,\nand whoever reads a half-extracted tree gets `ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND` for whichever file\nwas not written yet.\n\nuse-m's own recovery amplifies it: `installPackage()` runs\n`removePackageAlias(packagePath, 'incomplete')` after *every* failed attempt, so the\nloser recursively deletes the tree the winner just installed successfully — which is\nexactly how a process that saw no error at all ends up with `ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND`.\n\n**Retrying cannot help, because every retry re-enters the same race** — visible verbatim\nin the 2.11.1 log, where all three of use-m's attempts fail on the same path within nine\nseconds.\n\nReproduced three ways, all recorded under\n[`docs/case-studies/issue-2113/raw/`](docs/case-studies/issue-2113/raw):\n\n| Reproduction | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| npm alone, 24 concurrent installs of one alias | 22/24 fail, alias gone afterwards |\n| npm alone, 5 concurrent installs of *different* packages (control) | 0/5 fail |\n| stock use-m 8.14.4 from the CDN, 8 concurrent `use('command-stream')` | 2/8 fail |\n| end to end, 24 concurrent loads, unguarded | **24/24 fail in 54.8 s** |\n| end to end, 24 concurrent loads, guarded | **0/24 in 3.3 s** |\n\nThe control is what narrows the fix to a **per-alias** lock rather than a global install\nlock that would needlessly serialise unrelated dependencies.\n\n### Why the \"two commands\" workaround appeared to work\n\nThe issue reports that splitting the work into `/task --ci-cd` + `/claude <issue>` never\nfails. That is consistent with the race, not against it:\n[`measure-entrypoint-fanout.mjs`](experiments/issue-2113/measure-entrypoint-fanout.mjs)\nwalks each binary's import graph and counts reachable modules that top-level-await a\n`command-stream` load:\n\n```text\nbinary              modules   cold burst (static)   reachable (incl. dynamic)\ntask                93        6                     8\nfix                 95        6                     8\nreview              90        5                     9\nsolve               156       1                     25\n```\n\n`/task --ci-cd` never spawns anything — `src/telegram-task-command.lib.mjs:177` calls\n`createCiCdIssueFn()` in the already-warm bot process, so it installs nothing — and\n`/claude <issue>` spawns `solve`, whose cold burst is exactly one loader. The workaround\nreduced exposure; it did not remove it.\n\n## Fix\n\n`src/use-m-single-flight.lib.mjs` (new, Node built-ins only) wraps `use()` inside\n`ensureUseM()`, so all 72 files that bootstrap through it inherit three guards with no\ncall-site changes:\n\n1. **Per-specifier single flight** — a `Map` from specifier to the in-flight promise,\n   evicted on rejection so failures stay retryable. Collapses the whole cold wave into\n   one install.\n2. **In-process per-alias mutex** — a promise chain keyed by the *alias*\n   (`command-stream-v-latest`), so `use('command-stream')` and\n   `use('command-stream/foo')` serialise while unrelated packages still install in\n   parallel.\n3. **Cross-process advisory lock** — atomic `mkdir` on `<alias>.lock`, `utimes`\n   heartbeat, stale-steal and timeout, so a bot, a container and a CI step sharing one\n   npm prefix cannot collide either. Degrades to in-process-only if the lock root is\n   unusable; relocatable with `HIVE_MIND_USE_M_LOCK_DIR`. Built-ins (`use('fs')`) are\n   deduplicated but never take the lock.\n\n`useWithRetry()` is kept underneath as recovery for corruption that already exists — the\nnew layer is prevention, which retries could not provide.\n\nThe guard deliberately has no dependencies: it is the thing that makes loading\ndependencies safe, so it cannot itself require `proper-lockfile` or `p-memoize`. Their\ndesigns are followed (`mkdir` strategy, `mtime` staleness; `stale: 10000`/`update: stale/2`\ninformed `DEFAULT_STALE_MS = 15000`/`DEFAULT_HEARTBEAT_MS = 1000`), documented in\n[`research.md`](docs/case-studies/issue-2113/research.md).\n\n## Diagnostics\n\nBoth attached logs were produced **with `--verbose`** and contained no loader output at\nall, which is why the first analysis had to work backwards from a stack trace. Loader\ntracing now activates on `--verbose` as well as `HIVE_MIND_USE_M_DEBUG=1`:\n\n```text\n[use-m] use('command-stream') loading (alias command-stream-v-latest)\n[use-m] use('command-stream') joined an in-flight load (alias command-stream-v-latest)\n[use-m] use('command-stream') loaded in 2841ms\n```\n\n## Upstream\n\nReported to use-m as\n[link-foundation/use-m#70](https://github.com/link-foundation/use-m/issues/70) with a\nstandalone reproduction (no Hive Mind code), the downstream workaround, and a suggested\npatch: in-flight dedup, a cross-process install lock, not deleting the alias after a\nfailed attempt without holding that lock, and verifying after install instead of before.\nPreserved in\n[`upstream-use-m-concurrency-report.md`](docs/case-studies/issue-2113/upstream-use-m-concurrency-report.md).\n\n**Fixed upstream and released as `use-m@8.15.0`** (2026-07-31T20:23:48Z), which adopts\nthe suggested cross-process lock: `.use-m/<alias>.lock` taken with atomic `mkdir` plus an\n`utimes` heartbeat and stale-steal, and a `.use-m/<alias>.installed.json` marker written\nonly after `npm install` returns, which `isPackageInstalled()` now requires — closing the\ncheck-then-act window that let a caller import a half-extracted tree. Re-verified here\nwith the same standalone reproduction, 24 concurrent `use('command-stream')` calls on a\ncold prefix:\n\n| use-m | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| 8.14.4 | 22/24 fail in 58.3 s ([log](docs/case-studies/issue-2113/raw/experiment-upstream-use-m-8.14.4-control.log)) |\n| 8.15.0 | **0/24 in 3.5 s** ([log](docs/case-studies/issue-2113/raw/experiment-upstream-use-m-8.15.0-fixed.log)) |\n\nThe pinned CDN bootstrap fallback therefore moves from 8.14.4 to 8.15.0 (and the test's\nversion floor with it). The downstream guard is kept rather than removed: it is what still\nholds when the CDN serves an older bundle, and its per-specifier single flight collapses\nthe cold wave into *one* `npm install` instead of serialising several, which the upstream\nlock alone does not do.\n\nEarlier rounds of this issue produced\n[#66](https://github.com/link-foundation/use-m/issues/66) → `use-m@8.14.3` and\n[#68](https://github.com/link-foundation/use-m/issues/68) → `use-m@8.14.4`, both fixed and\nreleased. No use-m problem found in this issue is unreported. `command-stream` was audited\nand cleared — no report filed.\n\n## Tests\n\n- `tests/test-use-m-single-flight-2113.mjs` — 20 tests: dedup, alias serialisation,\n  parallelism across aliases, failure eviction, lock acquire/wait/steal/timeout/degrade,\n  idempotent wrapping, composition with the retry wrapper, and that `ensureUseM()`\n  installs the guard. Includes an **unguarded control** asserting that 35 of 36 concurrent\n  loads break without it.\n- Opt-in reproductions under `experiments/issue-2113/` (network + npm).\n\n```bash\nnode tests/test-use-m-single-flight-2113.mjs   # 20/20\nnpm run lint                                    # clean\nnpm test\n```\n\n## Case study\n\n[`docs/case-studies/issue-2113/`](docs/case-studies/issue-2113) holds every supplied log,\nthe workaround screenshot, the package/release audit, the recorded output of every\nexperiment, plus `timeline.md`, `requirements.md` (R1–R16, each mapped to evidence),\n`analysis.md` and `research.md`.\n\nFixes #2113\n\n",
