+{
+  "body": "## Summary\n\nThis pull request implements a solution for #2082: Check for all false positives, false negatives, warnings and errors in CI/CD and fix them all\n\n### Changes\n- 68 file(s) modified\n- 116881 line(s) added\n- 335 line(s) removed\n\n### Issue Reference\nFixes #2082\n\n---\n*This PR was created automatically by the AI issue solver*",
+  "commits": [
+    { "authoredDate": "2026-07-19T12:24:56Z", "authors": [{ "email": "drakonard@gmail.com", "id": "MDQ6VXNlcjE0MzE5MDQ=", "login": "konard", "name": "konard" }], "committedDate": "2026-07-19T12:24:56Z", "messageBody": "Adding .gitkeep for PR creation (default mode).\nThis file will be removed when the task is complete.\n\nIssue: https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/issues/2082", "messageHeadline": "Initial commit with task details", "oid": "efe9d3694e97ce1c898bc8cc0335198cb2fe0354" },
+    {
+      "authoredDate": "2026-07-19T12:45:16Z",
+      "authors": [{ "email": "drakonard@gmail.com", "id": "MDQ6VXNlcjE0MzE5MDQ=", "login": "konard", "name": "konard" }],
+      "committedDate": "2026-07-19T12:45:16Z",
+      "messageBody": "… nothing\n\nThe Helm Release job has been green on every run since 2025-12-23 while\npublishing nothing. The public chart repository is still at 0.38.8\n(2025-12-11) though package.json is at 2.8.3 — ~7 months of no-op releases\nreported as success.\n\nRoot cause: commit 5b7e60d7 converted helm-release.sh (which opened with\n`set -euo pipefail`) to .mjs using command-stream's `$`, which resolves with\n`.code` instead of throwing on a non-zero exit. The wrapping try/catch could\ntherefore never fire. In production `git checkout gh-pages` aborted because\ngh-pages also carries the repo tree and the script had just rewritten\nChart.yaml; the failure was swallowed, the index was committed onto main,\n`git push origin gh-pages` reported \"Everything up-to-date\", and the script\nprinted \"released successfully!\" and exited 0.\n\nVerified experimentally (experiments/issue-2082-command-stream-throw.mjs):\ncommand-stream's `$` returns code 1 without throwing.\n\n- add scripts/run-command.lib.mjs with runStrict(), restoring `set -e`\n  semantics on top of node:child_process (built-ins only, matching the\n  hardening already applied in publish-to-npm.mjs for #2028)\n- extract scripts/helm-release.lib.mjs so the flow is unit-testable\n- publish from an isolated `git worktree` instead of switching branches in\n  the dirty release checkout, removing the failure at its source\n- copy the archive next to index.yaml so its download URL resolves\n  (production emitted a .helm-packages/ URL that 404s)\n- verify the pushed index really contains the released version, so a future\n  regression is a red build rather than a silent no-op\n- add HIVE_MIND_CI_VERBOSE=1 command tracing, off by default\n\ntests/helm-release-2082.test.mjs pins all three properties. Confirmed by\nmutation: restoring fail-soft semantics fails the test with\n\"a failing git command must abort the release\".\n\nAlso compiles the full evidence pack and analysis into\ndev/log/issues/2082/pulls/2083/.\n\nRefs #2082",
+      "messageHeadline": "fix(ci): make Helm release fail loudly instead of silently publishing…",
+      "oid": "ba3c57a4807f117b7cfd6ef9b62dc6a7a3516ea3"
+    },
+    {
+      "authoredDate": "2026-07-19T12:51:40Z",
+      "authors": [{ "email": "drakonard@gmail.com", "id": "MDQ6VXNlcjE0MzE5MDQ=", "login": "konard", "name": "konard" }],
+      "committedDate": "2026-07-19T12:51:40Z",
+      "messageBody": "… (#2082)\n\nFinding F5 of issue #2082. Reproduced by tests/publish-verification-race-2082.test.mjs,\nwhich fails against the previous implementation.\n\nRoot cause: retry granularity. `publishWithRetry` verified the version with a\nsingle `npm view` immediately after publishing, and on a miss fell through to the\ntop of the retry loop — re-running the entire `npm run changeset:publish`. But the\nnpm registry serves metadata through a CDN, so a version can be invisible to\n`npm view` for seconds after publish returns success (npm/cli#3424, #9043, #593).\nA verification miss is therefore not evidence that the publish failed.\n\nObserved in CI run 29647956700, a run that went GREEN:\n\n    14:38:40  Publish attempt 1 of 3...\n    14:38:46  success packages published successfully: @link-assistant/hive-mind@2.8.3\n    14:38:46  Creating git tag... New tag: v2.8.3\n    14:38:46  npm error code E404                       <- verified 0.3s after publish\n    14:38:56  Publish attempt 2 of 3...                 <- re-ran the whole publish\n    14:38:59  error npm error You cannot publish over the previously published versions: 2.8.3.\n    14:39:09  Publish attempt 3 of 3...\n    14:39:10  Verified @link-assistant/hive-mind@2.8.3 is live on npm\n\nThe release had already succeeded on attempt 1. It went green only because a\nthird attempt fit inside the budget, and only because by then there were no\nchangesets left for attempt 3 to publish. With one more second of CDN lag, or\none fewer retry, a completed release would have been reported as a failure.\n\nChanges:\n  - waitForVersionOnRegistry(): polls `npm view` with exponential backoff\n    (2s -> 30s, ~90s total) instead of checking once.\n  - A publish that reported success is never re-run. If verification ultimately\n    fails the job fails — the #2028 false-positive protection is unchanged — but\n    it fails on the verification, not by publishing again.\n  - isVersionConflict(output, version): recognises npm's EPUBLISHCONFLICT for the\n    version being released and treats it as \"already landed\", still subject to\n    verification so a false claim cannot turn into a green release. Matched\n    before the non-retryable classifier, since npm reports the conflict with a\n    403 that would otherwise be misread as an auth failure.\n  - Genuine publish failures are still retried exactly as before.",
+      "messageHeadline": "fix(ci): stop the npm publish/verify race republishing a live version…",
+      "oid": "02498ca365f5bd194413be4f9d2776cb92b5f146"
+    },
+    {
+      "authoredDate": "2026-07-19T12:54:55Z",
+      "authors": [{ "email": "drakonard@gmail.com", "id": "MDQ6VXNlcjE0MzE5MDQ=", "login": "konard", "name": "konard" }],
+      "committedDate": "2026-07-19T12:54:55Z",
+      "messageBody": "… (#2082)\n\nFinding F4 of issue #2082 — the same fail-soft defect as F1, with a worse\nconsequence. Reproduced by tests/version-and-commit-2082.test.mjs, which fails\nagainst the previous implementation.\n\nRoot cause: scripts/version-and-commit.mjs ran every git command as a bare\n`await $\\`...\\``. command-stream's `$` resolves with `.code` instead of throwing,\nso the try/catch wrapping main() could never fire.\n\n`git push origin main` is racy by construction: the release workflow pushes the\nversion bump to main while merges and other runs push to the same branch. When\nthe push was rejected as non-fast-forward, the rejection was swallowed — the\nscript printed \"Version bump committed and pushed to main\", set\n`version_committed=true`, and exited 0. The downstream publish job then worked\nfrom a version that existed only in the runner's local checkout. Every command\nbefore the push had the same problem: a failed `npm run changeset:version` or\n`git commit` continued straight on to the push.\n\nChanges:\n  - scripts/version-and-commit.lib.mjs: the logic, extracted with injectable\n    runner/output/logger so it can be unit-tested, with every command run\n    through `runStrict` (restoring `set -e` semantics).\n  - pushWithRebaseRetry(): a rejected push is rebased onto the new remote HEAD\n    and retried (5 attempts, 3s apart) rather than assumed to have won the race.\n    isNonFastForward() distinguishes a lost race from auth/network/protected-\n    branch failures, which are not fixed by rebasing and so fail immediately\n    with the real error.\n  - `version_committed=true` is emitted only after a push that actually landed.\n  - scripts/version-and-commit.mjs is now a thin argument-parsing CLI and no\n    longer imports command-stream.\n\nVerified by mutation: restoring the fail-soft runner makes the test fail with\n\"Missing expected rejection: a failing version bump must abort instead of\ncontinuing to commit and push\"; replacing the retry with a single push makes the\nrejected-push case fail. Both are the production defects.",
+      "messageHeadline": "fix(ci): fail the release when the version bump is not pushed to main…",
+      "oid": "ec858696b8539694c240211e1e343c60a49782c0"
+    },
+    {
+      "authoredDate": "2026-07-19T12:58:06Z",
+      "authors": [{ "email": "drakonard@gmail.com", "id": "MDQ6VXNlcjE0MzE5MDQ=", "login": "konard", "name": "konard" }],
+      "committedDate": "2026-07-19T12:58:06Z",
+      "messageBody": "… (#2082)\n\nFinding F6 of issue #2082. 22 of the 26 jobs across the two workflows had no\n`timeout-minutes`, so each inherited GitHub's 360-minute default: a job that\nhangs on a prompt, a dead socket or a runaway test burns six hours of runner\ntime and then surfaces as a generic failure that looks nothing like a hang.\ndocs/CI-CD-BEST-PRACTICES.md already required an explicit timeout; nothing\nenforced it.\n\nTimeouts were chosen from measured job durations across the last four successful\nrelease runs (29685608578, 29686630893, 29686761617, 29686947280) rather than\nguessed. Observed maxima: test-suites 11m, Docker DinD amd64 7m, Docker amd64\n6m, everything else under 2m. Applied limits give roughly 2-3x headroom\n(test-suites 30, Docker merge jobs 30, release/instant-release 30, most checks\n10-15). The four matrix Docker jobs already had 60 and are unchanged.\n\nEnforcement lives in tests/ci-workflow-timeouts-2082.test.mjs, backed by\nscripts/workflow-lint.lib.mjs. Nothing off the shelf does this: the zizmor\nrequest (zizmorcore/zizmor#1023) and the actionlint request (rhysd/actionlint#49)\nare both still open, so this is a custom check until one of them ships.\n\nTwo correctness details the check gets right, both of which would otherwise\ntrade one false result for another:\n  - Jobs that call a reusable workflow via `uses:` are exempt. GitHub does not\n    support `timeout-minutes` there; the timeout must live in the called\n    workflow. Demanding one would be a false positive.\n  - A `uses:` on a *step* is an action, not a reusable-workflow call, so it must\n    not exempt the job. Confusing the two would silently exempt nearly every job\n    in this repository.\n\nThe scanner is line-based rather than a YAML parse because js-yaml is only a\ntransitive dependency here and these checks must survive an unknown\nnode_modules state — the same constraint that shaped scripts/run-command.lib.mjs.\nThe result was cross-checked against a real YAML parse: 26 jobs, none missing.\n\nThe test also guards against passing vacuously, asserting both that workflow\nfiles were found and that jobs were actually parsed.",
+      "messageHeadline": "fix(ci): require timeout-minutes on every job and enforce it in tests…",
+      "oid": "0e4eb5991085b8e24b89a4dea360874950691555"
+    },
+    {
+      "authoredDate": "2026-07-19T13:09:58Z",
+      "authors": [{ "email": "drakonard@gmail.com", "id": "MDQ6VXNlcjE0MzE5MDQ=", "login": "konard", "name": "konard" }],
+      "committedDate": "2026-07-19T13:09:58Z",
+      "messageBody": "…2082)\n\nscripts/test-auto-fork-option.sh reported none of its three assertions:\neach failed branch printed \"Could not verify ...\" and the script carried\non to exit 0.\n\nThis was not a latent risk. The marker the dry-run checks grep for first\nappears ~32s into the run, because solve.mjs makes several GitHub API\ncalls before reaching the auto-fork decision — and the script killed the\ncommand with `timeout 10s`. Both greps had been failing on every run\nwhile the job stayed green. Reproduced locally: restoring the 10s\ntimeout now fails the script with exactly those two checks red.\n\n- test-auto-fork-option.sh: realistic timeouts, every check asserts, and\n  a hermetic --help check that does not depend on the network.\n- check-file-line-limits.sh: a missing release.yml was a warning that\n  skipped the check entirely; it is now an error.\n- scripts/shell-lint.lib.mjs + tests: flag any `else` branch that reports\n  a failure without failing. Branches that record the failure for a later\n  check are exempt; deliberately non-fatal ones must say so with an\n  explicit `# shell-lint: allow-nonfatal` marker and a reason.\n\nShellCheck does not cover this — an `else` that only echoes is valid\nbash, so judging it needs repo-specific intent.",
+      "messageHeadline": "fix(ci): make shell test assertions fail the build, and enforce it (#…",
+      "oid": "5b9155c72021af78ac3bbeb735e3105a676799f8"
+    },
+    {
+      "authoredDate": "2026-07-19T13:15:55Z",
+      "authors": [{ "email": "drakonard@gmail.com", "id": "MDQ6VXNlcjE0MzE5MDQ=", "login": "konard", "name": "konard" }],
+      "committedDate": "2026-07-19T13:15:55Z",
+      "messageBody": "F14 — the concurrency policy was inverted:\n\n    cancel-in-progress: ${{ github.ref == 'refs/heads/main' }}\n\nIt cancelled in-progress runs on main — the one branch that publishes —\nand queued them everywhere else. A release cancelled between `npm\npublish` and the `git push` of the version bump leaves the registry\nahead of the repository: the same split-brain state as F4 and F5,\nreached by a different route. Now main queues and PR branches cancel,\nwhich is also where the wasted runners were.\n\nF13 — nine jobs guarded their `if:` with `always()`, which is true even\nafter a cancellation, so they defeated the policy above. release.yml\ndocuments this rule at the top of the file (from the #1274 and #1278\ncase studies) but applied it only to the Docker jobs. Five used bare\n`always()`; four paired it with a contradictory `!cancelled()`.\n\nVerified semantics with a real YAML parse: exactly 9 of 25 jobs changed,\neach only in the leading term, the other 16 byte-identical. Note that\n`if: !cancelled() && ...` must be wrapped in ${{ }} — a leading `!` is a\nYAML tag, and the previous always() spelling did not need this.\n\nAlso extracts two Docker blocks from release.yml into scripts/. The\ntimeout-minutes added in 0e4eb599 had pushed the file to 1512 lines,\nover the 1500-line limit — which no CI run caught, because the missing\nchangeset failed the changeset gate and every other job was skipped.\nThat changeset is added here too.",
+      "messageHeadline": "fix(ci): stop cancelling releases mid-publish, drop always() (#2082)",
+      "oid": "9a1cbf488b9101cd4f85b01fdb002b55310aa2a2"
+    },
+    {
+      "authoredDate": "2026-07-19T13:25:36Z",
+      "authors": [{ "email": "drakonard@gmail.com", "id": "MDQ6VXNlcjE0MzE5MDQ=", "login": "konard", "name": "konard" }],
+      "committedDate": "2026-07-19T13:25:36Z",
+      "messageBody": "…(#2082)\n\nThe docker build and container-verification commands moved out of\nrelease.yml into scripts/docker-pr-build.sh and\nscripts/docker-pr-verify-containers.sh so the workflow stays under the\n1500-line limit. Two contract tests asserted on the literal text inline in\nrelease.yml and failed.\n\nRather than dropping the assertions, they now follow the indirection: the\njob must invoke each script, AND each script must still contain the build\nand verify commands. Asserting only the 'bash scripts/...' line would pass\nagainst an empty script — verified by mutation: replacing\ndocker-pr-build.sh with a stub makes both tests fail.\n\nAlso adds the implementation log for issue #2082, recording the root cause,\nfix and mutation check for F1/F3/F4/F5/F6/F13/F14, plus the gate-masking\neffect that let a line-limit break reach main unnoticed.",
+      "messageHeadline": "test(ci): follow docker-pr-check script extraction in contract tests …",
+      "oid": "740cfe987e581bdfdcf8d2a8f6f6d80ddf610a58"
+    },
+    { "authoredDate": "2026-07-19T13:49:53Z", "authors": [{ "email": "drakonard@gmail.com", "id": "MDQ6VXNlcjE0MzE5MDQ=", "login": "konard", "name": "konard" }], "committedDate": "2026-07-19T13:49:53Z", "messageBody": "", "messageHeadline": "Add development log for issue #2082 PR #2083", "oid": "af57fdd6cc616745cf1ca23697ac1853e396c232" },
+    {
+      "authoredDate": "2026-07-19T14:14:39Z",
+      "authors": [{ "email": "drakonard@gmail.com", "id": "MDQ6VXNlcjE0MzE5MDQ=", "login": "konard", "name": "konard" }],
+      "committedDate": "2026-07-19T14:14:39Z",
+      "messageBody": "npm run lint and eslint.config.mjs both scoped linting to src/, scripts/\nand eslint-rules/. tests/ - the largest .mjs tree in the repo - was never\nlinted, so the lint job reported success while the test suite carried\nreal defects.\n\nEnabling the glob surfaced them across 59 files: assert.match regexes\ncontaining unescaped literal indentation, a catch that discarded the\noriginal error, and a batch of unused bindings. These were not\nhypothetical - they were live in tests CI runs on every push, and lint\nstayed green. A false negative of the kind this issue is about.\n\nAdds lint-covers-tests-2082.test.mjs pinning the coverage. It asserts\nboth npm run lint and the config files entry include each required\ntree, since CI reads the former while --fix and editors read the\nlatter; drift means one of them lies about what is checked.\n\nChange detection already gates lint on any .mjs change, so tests/ edits\ntrigger the job - no workflow change needed.",
+      "messageHeadline": "fix(ci): lint the tests/ tree and fix the defects it surfaced (#2082)",
+      "oid": "fd3a44ef3d5ac8fefe4869f9fb84ceea19631e5a"
+    },
+    {
+      "authoredDate": "2026-07-19T15:25:50Z",
+      "authors": [{ "email": "drakonard@gmail.com", "id": "MDQ6VXNlcjE0MzE5MDQ=", "login": "konard", "name": "konard" }],
+      "committedDate": "2026-07-19T15:25:50Z",
+      "messageBody": "…ell (#2082)\n\ngetClaudeEnv() builds the child environment by spreading process.env. For\nadaptive-thinking-only models it explicitly deletes an inherited\nMAX_THINKING_TOKENS, but nothing did the same for CLAUDE_CODE_EFFORT_LEVEL.\nThe block below it only ever assigns, so on every path that computes no\nlevel the parent's value survived into the child:\n\n  - a model supporting no effort levels at all (haiku), which skips the\n    enclosing supportsEffortLevel() branch entirely;\n  - --think off on a non-adaptive model, where neither inner branch fires.\n\nClaude Code exports this variable and hive-mind's agents run under Claude\nCode, so the leak fired in the ordinary case: 'haiku --think high'\ninherited effort=max for a model the codebase classifies as supporting\nnone. Fixed by mirroring the existing MAX_THINKING_TOKENS sanitisation.\n\nThis also made the suite return opposite verdicts for the same commit.\nCI runs without the variable and stayed green; run the same tests inside\nClaude Code and test-opus-47-model-support.mjs failed with 5 errors. It\nnow passes 166/0 under a polluted environment as well as a clean one.\n\nAdds effort-level-env-leak-2082.test.mjs, which pins the leak, the\nbehaviour that had to survive the fix (issue #2032's lowest-effort\nmapping for adaptive-only models on --think off), and the general\ninvariant that the result is independent of the ambient shell.\n\nThree sibling variables leak identically and are confirmed present in the\nagent environment (CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_1M_CONTEXT, CLAUDE_CODE_AUTO_COMPACT_WINDOW,\nCLAUDE_AUTOCOMPACT_PCT_OVERRIDE). Deliberately left alone: no sanitisation\nprecedent, no failing test, and plausibly intentional user config. Recorded\nin the dev log for an explicit decision rather than a silent change.\n\nAlso documents F21 (the tests/ lint gap), F22 (this leak) and F23 (a\npre-existing flake in test-issue-1209-overrides.mjs, root-caused to a\nlive unpkg.com fetch on every bot startup against a fixed 12s kill).",
+      "messageHeadline": "fix(config): stop CLAUDE_CODE_EFFORT_LEVEL leaking from the parent sh…",
+      "oid": "6c3419eb73e852121c19d391f74943a5023ace91"
+    },
+    {
+      "authoredDate": "2026-07-19T15:37:29Z",
+      "authors": [{ "email": "drakonard@gmail.com", "id": "MDQ6VXNlcjE0MzE5MDQ=", "login": "konard", "name": "konard" }],
+      "committedDate": "2026-07-19T15:37:29Z",
+      "messageBody": "Commit fd3a44ef silenced timers/no-leaked-timers on this helper with\nexitTimer.unref?.(). unref does not just make a timer cancellable - it\nremoves it from the handles keeping the event loop alive. That callback\nis what settles the awaited closeScreenSession, so Node exited with the\nawait still pending: exit 13 on CI (run 29690457864, Node 24), and a\nsilent pass locally on Node 20, where unsettled top-level await is not\ndetected at all.\n\nCapture the timer and clear it from inside its own callback instead. It\nstays referenced until it fires, cb(0) settles the await, and the handle\nis released - satisfying both the lint rule and the runtime.\n\nAudited the other six files whose timers that lint pass touched: unref\nwas added in exactly one place, and every added clearTimeout runs after\nthe awaited work, so it can only no-op on an already-fired timer.\n\nThe suite was re-run under Node 24 rather than the sandbox's Node 20,\nsince the project's own engines field requires >=24 and the older\nruntime cannot observe this failure mode.",
+      "messageHeadline": "fix(test): keep the fake spawn exit timer referenced (#2082)",
+      "oid": "1e04c45ec9e9e15cb03833b094f4f72f835ea9f5"
+    },
+    { "authoredDate": "2026-07-19T15:40:34Z", "authors": [{ "email": "drakonard@gmail.com", "id": "MDQ6VXNlcjE0MzE5MDQ=", "login": "konard", "name": "konard" }], "committedDate": "2026-07-19T15:40:34Z", "messageBody": "The changeset gate requires exactly one changeset added per PR, and run\n29692835784 failed on it with two. That gate runs before everything else,\nso test-suites and the rest of the checks were skipped rather than run -\nthe failure masked the state of every other job in the PR.\n\nFolds the tests/ lint coverage and the CLAUDE_CODE_EFFORT_LEVEL leak into\nthe existing CI/CD changeset. No content dropped.", "messageHeadline": "chore(changeset): combine this PR's two changesets into one (#2082)", "oid": "15888c1e314f41a6ea30876a827c3cad98b88b4b" },
+    {
+      "authoredDate": "2026-07-19T15:53:23Z",
+      "authors": [{ "email": "drakonard@gmail.com", "id": "MDQ6VXNlcjE0MzE5MDQ=", "login": "konard", "name": "konard" }],
+      "committedDate": "2026-07-19T15:53:23Z",
+      "messageBody": "F25: a PR can report green with its own code never executed by CI.\ndetect-code-changes.mjs diffs the latest push (before..after) on\nsynchronize, while branch protection reads the latest run - so a\ndocs-only commit pushed after a failed run leaves the heavy jobs\nskipped and the PR green. Demonstrated on this PR: fd3a44ef ran\ntest-suites and failed, 1e04c45e carried the fix but was skipped\nbehind the changeset gate, and 15888c1e (changesets only) reported\nsuccess with the fix never executed. Not fixed here - every option\noverturns behaviour issue #1665 introduced and pinned with a test,\nwhich is a maintainer decision, so it is recorded with run IDs and\nverbatim output instead.\n\nR6: the previous claim - that the template lacks detectPublishFailure()\n/ FAILURE_PATTERNS - was wrong, and is retracted. It came from reading\nonly publish-failure-classifier.mjs; the template defines both locally\nin publish-to-npm.mjs. Filed the claim that does hold up instead:\ntemplate#105, the F5 publish/verify race, confirmed against the live\nsource and reported with a deterministic reproduction.\n\nF23: a second instance, tests/test-hive-no-silent-failure.mjs, with a\n5s budget against 7-9s CDN-bound startup. Confirmed pre-existing (3/3\nfailures on origin/main). CI's use-m warm-up step reduces but does not\nremove it, which is the evidence that only local resolution fixes it.",
+      "messageHeadline": "docs(2082): record F25, correct the R6 upstream claim, extend F23",
