+    "body": "## Summary\n\nIn `.github/workflows/release.yml`, both manual-dispatch jobs are declared with **no `needs:` at all**:\n\n```yaml\n  instant-release:\n    name: Instant Release\n    if: github.event_name == 'workflow_dispatch' && github.event.inputs.release_mode == 'instant'\n```\n\n```yaml\n  changeset-pr:\n    name: Create Changeset PR\n    if: github.event_name == 'workflow_dispatch' && github.event.inputs.release_mode == 'changeset-pr'\n```\n\n(lines 527-529 and 646-648 at the current `main`.)\n\nBecause a job with no `needs:` starts immediately and in parallel with every other job, `instant-release` runs its full publish sequence — version bump, commit to `main`, `npm publish` via OIDC trusted publishing, GitHub release — while `lint` and `test` are still running, or even after they have failed. The workflow run is then marked failed, but the package is already on the registry and the version commit is already on `main`. npm does not allow republishing that version, so recovery means a deprecate/unpublish plus another bump.\n\nThis contradicts the pipeline's own best-practice #9 (\"validated releases only\"), which every other release path in the template does follow: the automatic `release` job is correctly gated on `needs: [lint, test]`.\n\n## Reproduction\n\n1. Generate a repository from this template.\n2. Introduce a deliberate failure, e.g. add `const x =;` to a source file, or make a unit test assert `false`.\n3. Push it to `main`.\n4. Run the workflow manually: **Actions -> Release -> Run workflow**, with `release_mode: instant` and `bump_type: patch`.\n\nObserved: `lint` and `test` go red, and `instant-release` publishes the broken code to npm anyway.\nExpected: `instant-release` never starts once a quality gate has failed.\n\n## Workaround\n\nOnly trigger `release_mode: instant` from a commit whose CI is already green, and check the run of that exact SHA first. This is a policy, not a control — nothing in the workflow enforces it.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nAdd the dependency and gate on the dependency *results*, not merely on job completion. `needs:` alone is not sufficient here: `detect-changes` is skipped on `workflow_dispatch`, and a skipped dependency skips its dependents unless the condition survives it (see [actions/runner#491](https://github.com/actions/runner/issues/491)). `!cancelled()` keeps the condition evaluable while still stopping the job when the run is cancelled.\n\n```yaml\n  instant-release:\n    name: Instant Release\n    needs: [lint, test]\n    if: >-\n      !cancelled() &&\n      github.event_name == 'workflow_dispatch' &&\n      github.event.inputs.release_mode == 'instant' &&\n      needs.lint.result == 'success' &&\n      needs.test.result == 'success'\n```\n\n```yaml\n  changeset-pr:\n    name: Create Changeset PR\n    needs: [lint]\n    # A changeset PR does not publish, so it only waits on the fast lint gate.\n    if: >-\n      !cancelled() &&\n      github.event_name == 'workflow_dispatch' &&\n      github.event.inputs.release_mode == 'changeset-pr' &&\n      needs.lint.result == 'success'\n```\n\nNote the block scalar: `if: !cancelled() && ...` written on a single line is invalid YAML, because a plain scalar may not begin with `!` (the tag indicator).\n\n## Context\n\nFound while auditing CI/CD false positives and false negatives in a repository seeded from this template: link-foundation/browser-commander#75. The fix above is applied there in link-foundation/browser-commander#76.",
