+    "body": "Found while auditing a downstream consumer of the Rust sibling of this template (`link-assistant/formal-ai`, issue [#808](https://github.com/link-assistant/formal-ai/issues/808)). This template is the best of the three on every other axis — it is the only one with `simulate-fresh-merge.sh`, `secretlint`, and clean `!cancelled()` guards — but it shares one gap.\n\n## The Dockerfile is never built at pull-request stage\n\n`docker-publish` (`.github/workflows/release.yml:552`) is gated on the release jobs:\n\n```yaml\n  docker-publish:\n    name: Optional Docker Hub Publish\n    needs: [release, instant-release]\n    if: |\n      !cancelled() &&\n      (\n        (needs.release.result == 'success' && needs.release.outputs.published == 'true') ||\n        (needs.instant-release.result == 'success' && needs.instant-release.outputs.published == 'true')\n      )\n```\n\nSo the image is only ever built *after* the package has been published. A Dockerfile regression cannot fail a pull request; it produces a half-finished release instead — package published, no image, no image-bearing GitHub Release. We hit exactly that downstream (runs 29312084458 and 29485000765).\n\n**Suggested fix** — a build-only pull-request job. `push: false, load: true` keeps it working for fork pull requests, which have no registry credentials, and the GHA layer cache keeps the common case off a cold build:\n\n```yaml\n  docker-build:\n    name: Docker Image Build Check\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    timeout-minutes: 30\n    needs: [detect-changes]\n    if: github.event_name == 'pull_request' && needs.detect-changes.outputs.any-code-changed == 'true'\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v6\n      - uses: docker/setup-buildx-action@v4\n      - uses: docker/build-push-action@v7\n        with:\n          context: .\n          push: false\n          load: true\n          tags: app:pr-check\n          cache-from: type=gha\n          cache-to: type=gha,mode=max\n```\n\nIf the repo also ships a runtime-contract script baked into the image, run it here too (`docker run --rm app:pr-check <verifier>`) — that is what we did downstream, where the verifier existed but had no caller anywhere in CI.\n\n**Workaround** without a new job: add `docker build .` to an existing pull-request job, at the cost of not sharing the buildx layer cache with the publish build.\n\nCompanion reports for the siblings, which have additional gaps this template does not: link-foundation/rust-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template#100, link-foundation/python-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template#35.\n\nHappy to open a pull request for this if useful.\n",
