+    "body": "## Summary\n\n`scripts/publish-to-npm.mjs` treats \"publish succeeded\" and \"verification succeeded\" as a single retryable unit. When the publish itself works but the post-publish verification misses because npm's registry has not propagated yet, the retry loop re-runs the **entire** `changeset publish`. The republish then legitimately fails with \"cannot publish over the previously published version\", so a fully successful release can be reported as a failure — or succeed only by luck, if a later attempt happens to fit inside the retry budget.\n\nFound while auditing the same failure mode downstream in `link-assistant/hive-mind` ([issue #2082](https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/issues/2082)), where it was observed in a real, **green** release run.\n\n## Where\n\n`scripts/publish-to-npm.mjs`\n\n`attemptPublish()` verifies after a fixed 2-second sleep, and returns a plain failure if the version is not visible yet:\n\n```js\nconsole.log('Verifying package was published to npm...');\nawait sleep(2000); // Wait for npm registry to propagate\nconst isPublished = await verifyPublished(packageName, currentVersion);\n\nif (isPublished) {\n  return { success: true, error: null };\n}\n\nconsole.error('Verification failed: package not found on npm after publish');\nreturn {\n  success: false,\n  error: new Error('Package not found on npm after publish attempt'),\n};\n```\n\nThat error is not marked `nonRetryable`, so `main()`'s loop retries it — and the retry calls `attemptPublish` again, which re-runs `changeset publish` from the top:\n\n```js\nfor (let i = 1; i <= MAX_RETRIES; i++) {\n  const { success, error } = await attemptPublish(/* ... */);\n  if (success) { /* ... */ return; }\n  if (error?.nonRetryable) { /* fail fast */ }\n  if (i < MAX_RETRIES) await sleep(RETRY_DELAY);\n}\n```\n\n## Why 2 seconds is not enough\n\nnpm serves package metadata from a CDN with read replicas. Propagation after a successful publish is routinely a few seconds and occasionally far longer; it is not bounded by any published SLA. A single check at a fixed offset therefore samples a race. The downstream occurrence verified ~0.3s after publishing and got an E404 for a package that had, in fact, just been published successfully.\n\n## Sequence observed downstream\n\n```\nattempt 1 -> changeset publish  -> success\n             verify             -> E404 (registry had not propagated)\nattempt 2 -> changeset publish  -> \"cannot publish over the previously published versions\"\nattempt 3 -> nothing left to publish; verification finally passed\n```\n\nThe run went green, but only because attempt 3 landed inside `MAX_RETRIES`. With one more second of propagation lag the same successful publish would have reported a hard failure.\n\n## Reproduction\n\nDeterministic, no registry needed — inject a runner whose publish always succeeds and whose verification misses the first two times:\n\n```js\nlet publishes = 0;\nlet checks = 0;\nconst runner = async cmd => {\n  if (cmd.includes('changeset publish')) {\n    publishes++;\n    if (publishes > 1) throw new Error('cannot publish over the previously published versions');\n    return { code: 0, stdout: 'success', stderr: '' };\n  }\n  // npm view: miss twice, then hit — i.e. normal propagation lag\n  if (++checks < 3) throw new Error('npm error code E404');\n  return { code: 0, stdout: '1.0.0', stderr: '' };\n};\n```\n\nExpected: one publish, verification eventually succeeds, exit 0.\nActual: a second `changeset publish` is attempted and fails on the conflict.\n\n## Workaround\n\nRaise `sleep(2000)` before verification. This only widens the race window — it does not remove it, and it slows every successful release to pay for a rare one.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nSeparate the two failure domains. A publish that reported success must never be repeated; the only correct response to a verification miss is **to look again**.\n\n1. Replace the single check with bounded polling (exponential backoff, e.g. 7 attempts from 2s up to 30s).\n2. If polling is exhausted, fail the release — but do **not** re-enter the publish path. Return a failure that the retry loop will not act on by republishing.\n3. Retry `changeset publish` only when the publish command itself failed.\n4. Treat `EPUBLISHCONFLICT` / \"already published\" as a cue to verify, not to fail.\n\nReference implementation, now in `link-assistant/hive-mind` at `scripts/publish-to-npm.mjs`: `waitForVersionOnRegistry()` for the polling, and `publishWithRetry()` for the split between the two domains. It keeps the \"never trust the publish command alone\" property — a publish that falsely claims success still fails the release, because verification is still required.\n\nI'm happy to open a PR porting that structure here if it's welcome.\n",
