+    "body": "## Summary\n\n`extractReleaseNotes()` builds the version-header regex without an **end anchor** after the version, so extracting notes for `1.2` can match the section that actually belongs to `1.2.3` (a prefix match).\n\n## Location\n\n`scripts/create-github-release.mjs:95-96`\n\n```js\nconst versionHeaderRegex = new RegExp(\n  `## ${escapeRegex(version)}[\\\\s\\\\S]*?(?=## \\\\d|$)`\n);\n```\n\nThe `escapeRegex()` call (added to neutralize the `.` wildcard) is correct, but `## 1\\.2` still matches at the start of a `## 1.2.3` header because nothing requires the version token to end.\n\n## Reproduction\n\nCHANGELOG:\n\n```md\n## 1.2.3\n- newer notes\n\n## 1.2\n- older notes\n```\n\n`extractReleaseNotes(changelog, '1.2')` returns the **1.2.3** notes (it matches starting at `## 1.2.3`), publishing the wrong section for the `1.2` release.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nAnchor the end of the version token:\n\n```js\nconst versionHeaderRegex = new RegExp(\n  `## ${escapeRegex(version)}(?=\\\\s|$)[\\\\s\\\\S]*?(?=## \\\\d|$)`\n);\n```\n\n(The Rust template's `create-github-release.rs` is immune — it uses `(?m)^## \\[{escaped}\\]`, bracket-delimited and line-anchored.)\n\n## Notes\n\nFound while auditing the downstream `link-assistant/web-search` release pipeline (issue #17). The same unanchored pattern exists there and is being fixed; the Python/C# templates that derive their changelog extraction from this script likely share it.\n",
