+    "body": "## Support multi-language monorepos: auto-detect layout, namespace releases, and badge the artifact\n\nWhen a repository hosts more than one language (e.g. `js/` + `rust/` in one monorepo, each driven by its template's release workflow), the per-language releases collide in a single flat namespace and the GitHub release does not link to the artifact it published. Discovered while shipping [link-assistant/web-search#15](https://github.com/link-assistant/web-search/issues/15); full write-up in [PR #16](https://github.com/link-assistant/web-search/pull/16) (`docs/case-studies/issue-15`).\n\n### Request\n\nMake the release job adapt with **no configuration**:\n\n1. **Auto-detect layout.** Manifest at the repo root → single-language; manifest in a language subdirectory (`js/package.json`, `js/package.json`, `rust/Cargo.toml`, …) → multi-language.\n   ```bash\n   if [ -f package.json ]; then ROOT=.; MULTI=false; else ROOT=js; MULTI=true; fi\n   ```\n2. **Namespace the tag** in multi-language mode: `js_v<version>` (keep a plain `v<version>` single-language).\n3. **Prefix the release title** `[JavaScript] <version>` in multi-language mode (`<name> <version>` single-language).\n4. **Badge the published artifact** in the release body — a shields.io badge linking to the exact version page: `https://www.npmjs.com/package/<pkg>/v/<version>`. Build the link from the **bare** version (strip any `v`/`lang_v` prefix first) so it resolves to the version page rather than 404ing.\n\nA small, unit-tested helper keeps tag/title construction **idempotent** (re-running on an already-prefixed version must not double-prefix).\n\n### Reference implementation\n\n`js/scripts/release-naming.mjs` (+ `release-naming.test.js`, 21 cases) and the `version` step of `.github/workflows/rust.yml` in [link-assistant/web-search#16](https://github.com/link-assistant/web-search/pull/16).\n",
