+    "body": "## Summary\n\n`scripts/create-github-release.mjs` sets the GitHub Release `name` field to the tag value, so for a multi-language repo using `--tag-prefix \"js-v\"` the resulting release titles look like `js-v0.3.5` instead of a human-readable `[JavaScript] 0.3.5`.\n\n## Evidence\n\nIn the current template:\n\nhttps://github.com/link-foundation/js-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template/blob/main/scripts/create-github-release.mjs#L82-L83\n\n```js\ntag_name: tag,\nname: tag,\n```\n\nA real release produced by this code: `https://github.com/link-foundation/lino-objects-codec/releases/tag/js-v0.3.5` has `tag_name: js-v0.3.5` and `name: js-v0.3.5`.\n\n## Expected Behavior\n\nThe release `name` should be a human-readable title independent of the tag prefix, e.g. `[JavaScript] 0.3.5`.\n\n## Suggested Fix\n\nAdd a `--language` option (default `JavaScript`) and a small helper that strips the language/`v` prefix from the version, then build the title as `[\\${language}] \\${semver}`. The fix used in the consumer repo:\n\nhttps://github.com/link-foundation/lino-objects-codec/pull/34\n\n## Related\n\nSame template's `format-release-notes-helpers.mjs` already has `normalizeReleaseVersionForBadge`, so the badge link in `format-release-notes.mjs` is already correct. This issue is only about the release-creation script's title.\n\nTracking issue in the consumer repository: https://github.com/link-foundation/lino-objects-codec/issues/33",
